If you learned Next.js routing before version 13, most of what you know is now wrong. Not “slightly different” — the imports moved, the <Link> API changed, and the <a> tag you were told to always include will now throw. I found this out the hard way porting an app that wrapped every <Link> around an <a>: the build failed on every single one.
The routing concepts survived. Dynamic segments, prefetching, programmatic navigation — all still there. But the surface is different enough that copy-pasting an old tutorial gets you a broken build. Here’s the current version, all of it compiled and run on Next.js 16.
Dynamic routes moved to app/ and params is a Promise
The old pages/user/[username].js with useRouter().query is gone. In the App Router the folder is app/user/[username]/page.tsx, the component is a server component, and you read the param off params — which is now a Promise you await.
// app/user/[username]/page.tsx
export default async function UserProfile({
params,
}: {
params: Promise<{ username: string }>;
}) {
const { username } = await params;
return <h1>Profile of {username}</h1>;
}I hit /user/johndoe on the running server and got <h1>Profile of johndoe</h1> in the server-rendered HTML — no client hydration needed to read the param. The Promise wrapper surprised me the first time; if you destructure params synchronously the types won't compile, and that's deliberate. It's how Next.js lets the param resolve without blocking the render.
There’s no useRouter().query for route params anymore either. If you need a param inside a client component, it's useParams() from next/navigation, not next/router. That import path is the single most common thing people get wrong — next/router still exists for the Pages Router, so the wrong import doesn't error loudly, it just returns nothing.
Link no longer takes a child anchor
This is the breaking change that’ll light up your whole codebase. The old pattern:
// DON'T — this throws in the App Router
<Link href="/about">
<a className="nav">About</a>
</Link><Link> renders its own <a> now. You put the props and children directly on it:
// app/components/nav-link.tsx
"use client";import Link from "next/link";
import { usePathname } from "next/navigation";
import type { ComponentProps } from "react";type NavLinkProps = ComponentProps<typeof Link>;export function NavLink({ className, ...props }: NavLinkProps) {
const pathname = usePathname();
const isActive = pathname === props.href; return (
<Link
{...props}
aria-current={isActive ? "page" : undefined}
className={`${className ?? ""} ${isActive ? "active" : ""}`.trim()}
/>
);
}This is the pattern I reach for constantly: a NavLink that knows whether it's the current page. usePathname() gives you the current path, you compare, you set aria-current and an active class. On the rendered /dashboard page the active link came back as aria-current="page" class="active" href="/dashboard" — correct, and accessible for free because screen readers announce aria-current.
Typing it as ComponentProps<typeof Link> means you inherit every prop <Link> accepts, including the typed-routes checking, without maintaining your own prop list.
Prefetch control still works, and you still want it
Next.js prefetches links in the viewport in production. Usually great. But if you’ve got a link to something huge and rarely clicked — an export page, a settings screen nobody visits — prefetching it wastes bandwidth on load.
<Link href="/blog/shipping-fast" prefetch={false}>
Changelog (no prefetch)
</Link>One caveat that trips people up in dev: prefetching only runs in production builds. If you’re testing prefetch behavior with next dev, you'll see nothing prefetch either way. Build and next start to see the real behavior. The link itself rendered fine with the flag set — Next.js just skips the background fetch.
Programmatic navigation: useRouter, but from the right place
Redirecting after a form submit still uses useRouter. Again — from next/navigation, in a client component. The API is push, replace, back, prefetch.
// app/search/page.tsx
"use client";import { useRouter } from "next/navigation";
import { useState } from "react";export default function Search() {
const router = useRouter();
const [q, setQ] = useState(""); function submit() {
router.push(`/blog/${encodeURIComponent(q)}`);
} return (
<div>
<input value={q} onChange={(e) => setQ(e.target.value)} />
<button onClick={submit}>Go</button>
</div>
);
}Note the encodeURIComponent — pushing raw user input into a path is how you get broken URLs and, occasionally, an open redirect. Encode it.
Page transitions with framer-motion
Next.js still doesn’t ship page transitions. framer-motion (now published as motion, but the framer-motion package still works) is the usual answer. The trick in the App Router is keying the wrapper on usePathname() so the animation actually re-runs when the route changes:
// app/components/page-transition.tsx
"use client";import { motion } from "framer-motion";
import { usePathname } from "next/navigation";
import type { ReactNode } from "react";export function PageTransition({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
const pathname = usePathname(); return (
<motion.div
key={pathname}
initial={{ opacity: 0, y: 8 }}
animate={{ opacity: 1, y: 0 }}
transition={{ duration: 0.25 }}
>
{children}
</motion.div>
);
}Without the key={pathname}, React reuses the same DOM node across navigations and the initial state never fires again — you get one animation on first load and nothing after. This built and rendered clean on framer-motion 12 with React 19.
The short version
Three imports to burn into memory: next/navigation (not next/router) for useRouter, usePathname, useParams. Two API changes to internalize: <Link> renders its own anchor, and dynamic-route params is a Promise you await. Get those and everything else is the routing you already knew, just in the app/ folder.
The migration itself is mechanical but tedious — mostly deleting <a> tags and fixing imports. Do it with a codemod if the project is big; do it by hand and you'll miss one and spend an hour finding it.
